plugins.description:
Decodes the downlink of a TETRA cell from raw I/Q (ETSI EN 300 392-2: pi/4-DQPSK, 18 ksymbol/s, 25 kHz continuous transmission) and reports what the network broadcasts about itself:

- the cell's identity from the BSCH synchronization PDU: country code (MCC), network code (MNC), colour code, and the timeslot, frame and multiframe numbers;
- the SYSINFO broadcast on the BNCH: main carrier, frequency band, offset, duplex spacing, location area, and the security and encryption flags the cell carries;
- the AACH access assignment, which says whether a slot is control or traffic;
- MAC-RESOURCE channel grants, with the granted address and whether the grant is encrypted.

IT PLAYS NO AUDIO: IT DECODES NO VOICE AND DECRYPTS NOTHING. A traffic channel carrying speech is detected and labelled 'voice - not decoded (the TETRA speech codec needs ETSI's permission, which FoxSDR does not have)'. The TETRA speech codec (ETSI EN 300 395) is not present - no ACELP, no ETSI reference code - and no TEA or other decryption is implemented; an encrypted transmission is shown as encrypted and nothing more.

Only TETRA decodes here. If no TETRA cell has synchronised after 10 seconds of signal, the panel says so: TETRA is pi/4-DQPSK in 25 kHz channels, and an analogue FM or DMR signal on the same band will not decode.

Written clean-room from ETSI EN 300 392-2 V3.8.1 alone; no third-party TETRA decoder was read. Every transcribed constant - the CRC block code, the RCPC puncturing, the block interleaving, the scrambling, the Reed-Muller code - is checked in the plugin's own test against a property the standard states independently, so a slip breaks a relationship rather than mis-decoding silently. Version 0.1.2: verified against a synthetic transmitter built from the standard, and NOT yet against a real cell off air.

0.1.2 FIXES A CRASH. With a radio open at a very high sample rate (30.72 MS/s was reported) FoxSDR could close the moment the radio opened, even with this plugin's window shut, because every loaded I/Q plugin is fed the whole band. The plugin now works only at receiver sample rates from 40 kS/s to 9.072 MS/s; at any other rate it does nothing and its text and panel say why. A rate above that range, 10 MS/s included, used to run and is now refused: the filter that narrows the band does not separate neighbouring carriers there. Lower the receiver's sample rate to use it; a TETRA channel is only 25 kHz wide. The decoding itself is otherwise unchanged. Version 0.1.1 named the plugin 'TETRA Monitor (data only, no voice)' inside FoxSDR, says first that it plays no audio, and says when no TETRA cell is found.
